WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:03.140
- I've worked the night shift
many times in my career.

00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:04.500
So, I'm excited to watch this show.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:05.750
Are you ready to rock?

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:06.583
Let's roll.

00:00:06.583 --> 00:00:09.331
(upbeat R&amp;B music)

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:13.740
- We're losin' him.
- Do something!

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.380
- I can't stop the bleeding.
- Take out the branch!

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:17.330
- [Doctor] I can't take out the branch.

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:18.163
He'll bleed out.

00:00:18.163 --> 00:00:19.186
- That's good advice.

00:00:19.186 --> 00:00:21.390
(laughs) You never wanna
take out any object

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:24.940
that's inserted into you
or gets impaled into you.

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:27.210
Taking out the branch is a bad idea.

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:29.900
If someone's bleeding out,
the first thing you wanna do

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:32.080
is apply as much pressure as possible.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:34.460
If that's not working, you
have to create a tourniquet.

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:35.970
And a tourniquet can
be made from anything.

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:39.170
You can rip off a shirt sleeve
and wrap it around the area.

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:41.860
Just remember, you have
to go closer to the body

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:44.410
with the tourniquet
from where the wound is.

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:46.380
So, for example, if the
wound is right here,

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:48.473
you wanna put the tourniquet on here.

00:00:48.473 --> 00:00:49.306
- A streamliner is cut.

00:00:49.306 --> 00:00:50.690
I'm gonna clamp it.

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:51.523
We're gonna use his blood.

00:00:51.523 --> 00:00:52.410
Start a line.

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:53.243
Go, go, go, go!

00:00:53.243 --> 00:00:56.180
- Maybe this is something you learn

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:58.130
when you're learning about war trauma.

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:00.070
This is not something
I've ever been taught.

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:01.480
This sounds ludicrous.

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:03.020
It looks ludicrous.

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:04.680
It's giving me the heebie jeebies.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:05.603
(chuckles)

00:01:05.603 --> 00:01:08.590
I hope no one does this as an EMT.

00:01:08.590 --> 00:01:10.484
- Holy crap, it's working!

00:01:10.484 --> 00:01:13.040
- Where'd you learn that?
- Afghanistan.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:13.873
- See?

00:01:13.873 --> 00:01:14.706
Afghanistan.

00:01:14.706 --> 00:01:15.539
He's a war guy.

00:01:15.539 --> 00:01:16.424
- Hi.

00:01:16.424 --> 00:01:17.257
(loud gasp)

00:01:17.257 --> 00:01:18.090
I'm Dr. Alexander and
I'm going to deactivate

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:19.050
your defibrillator with a magnet.

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:20.560
- Jordan, this is my patient!

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:21.650
- You should have been
the one helping her.

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:22.640
- If anything happens, we're legally--

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:24.090
- The only thing that's going to happen

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:26.170
is that you are going to
stop feeling like a horse

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:27.360
is kicking you in the chest, okay?

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:28.503
It's perfectly safe.

00:01:28.503 --> 00:01:30.870
(labored breathing)

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:31.703
There we go.

00:01:31.703 --> 00:01:34.820
- I'm not 100% familiar with
pacemakers and defibrillators

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:37.040
and how to turn them off.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:39.840
There are reps onsite within my hospital

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:41.560
that we do call if there's a malfunction.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:44.390
I've never had a defibrillator
that would shock a patient

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:45.750
repeatedly over and over again.

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:46.583
That's never happened to me.

00:01:46.583 --> 00:01:48.890
I didn't know about the magnet trick.

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:50.250
It's kinda cool.

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:53.230
I need to look into that further
because I guess this could

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:55.160
happen to one of my patients
and if I have a magnet

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:56.950
lying around, I should help them.

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:58.570
- You got real lucky
taking that branch out.

00:01:58.570 --> 00:01:59.840
- Luck had nothing to do with it, Hank.

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:01.370
- I heard about it over dispatch.

00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:02.830
That's a hell of a way
to start your shift.

00:02:02.830 --> 00:02:03.820
- Wouldn't have been my first choice.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:04.653
- Geez, T.C.

00:02:04.653 --> 00:02:05.486
You look like crap.

00:02:05.486 --> 00:02:06.790
- Yeah, getting a tree
out of a guy's stomach

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:07.793
tends to be a little messy.

00:02:07.793 --> 00:02:08.626
- (laughs) Okay.

00:02:08.626 --> 00:02:11.280
We don't just change in
the middle of the hospital

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:12.740
and start showing off.

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:14.660
Well, this guy is showing off his body.

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:16.760
I love these dramatic
shows and they make it fun

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:19.130
and interesting to watch,
but okay, not realistic.

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:20.720
I have to call that out right away.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:21.916
- I'm done being nice about this.

00:02:21.916 --> 00:02:23.830
- (laughs) This is nice.

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:24.663
- You know what?

00:02:24.663 --> 00:02:26.504
I am going to ride you
until I get that paperwork--

00:02:26.504 --> 00:02:27.960
- (groans) You have a boyfriend, Jordan,

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:29.441
so sex with me is not gonna happen.

00:02:29.441 --> 00:02:30.274
(winces)

00:02:30.274 --> 00:02:31.210
- I don't know if this
is a complete rip-off

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:32.043
of "Grey's Anatomy",

00:02:32.043 --> 00:02:34.240
but he really reminds
me of Patrick Dempsey.

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:35.420
I don't know if you guys agree but.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:36.960
- We met last year at
the intern interviews.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:37.793
Talked about the MCATs.

00:02:37.793 --> 00:02:39.700
You scored a 34, right?
- 35.

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:42.020
- Oh, I got, like, a 35.6.

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:42.853
- Good for you.

00:02:42.853 --> 00:02:44.670
- The way the MCAT works when I took it,

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:47.980
it was a three-part test.

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:50.470
For example, if you
got 10s on one section,

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:52.820
10 on another section,
those actually you get a 30.

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:54.460
I didn't know you could
get decimal points.

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:56.680
I guess that's something new?

00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:58.620
- I wouldn't be doing that, new guy.

00:02:58.620 --> 00:02:59.453
- No worries, man.

00:02:59.453 --> 00:03:00.286
I know what I'm doing, all right?

00:03:00.286 --> 00:03:02.020
I can juggle anything.

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:03.490
Even junk from the lost and found.

00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:04.440
- Yeah.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:05.710
Yeah, that's not lost and found.

00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:07.030
Those are things the doc's been takin' out

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:08.635
of people's rectums this month.

00:03:08.635 --> 00:03:09.468
(laughs)

00:03:09.468 --> 00:03:10.301
(groans)

00:03:10.301 --> 00:03:11.261
(ball squeaks)

00:03:11.261 --> 00:03:12.400
(laughing)

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:13.233
- [Krista] I'd wash my
hands if I were you.

00:03:13.233 --> 00:03:15.410
- I wanna be seen now!
- Sir, I--

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:16.704
- I said now, (bleep)!

00:03:16.704 --> 00:03:17.537
- Whoa!

00:03:17.537 --> 00:03:18.370
Hey, hey!

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:19.203
- Sir, you're gonna have to--!

00:03:19.203 --> 00:03:20.036
(shouting)

00:03:20.036 --> 00:03:20.869
- Hey, calm down!

00:03:20.869 --> 00:03:21.702
(landing punches)

00:03:21.702 --> 00:03:22.535
- Hey, hey, hey.

00:03:22.535 --> 00:03:23.368
Bubba, bubba, hey, calm down, all right?

00:03:23.368 --> 00:03:24.367
We don't want any trouble here.

00:03:24.367 --> 00:03:26.960
(yelling in pain)

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:28.710
- (laughs) All right, this would not fly.

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:30.370
Patients go wild like this.

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:31.203
It happens.

00:03:31.203 --> 00:03:34.780
Especially those under the
influence of drugs, alcohol,

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:35.613
and that sort of thing.

00:03:35.613 --> 00:03:37.500
This is heavily under reported.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:41.170
Healthcare worker abuse,
whether that's nurses, doctors,

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:44.510
PAs, pharmacists, whoever
it may be, get abused often

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:47.610
and often meaning more than it should.

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:51.020
And it doesn't raise any headlines.

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:53.010
It doesn't raise any
alarms, but it needs to.

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:55.620
We shouldn't have to have
patients yelling at us

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:58.290
and punching us and threatening
us and throwing things down

00:03:58.290 --> 00:03:59.500
because we're there to help people.

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:01.210
But, what this doctor's
doing is also uncool.

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:02.060
Stop choking people.

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:03.060
You're gonna hurt someone for real.

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:04.350
- You admitted her.

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:06.450
I told 'em to transfer her back to Corpus.

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:08.700
- I determined that she wasn't
stable enough to travel.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:10.090
- She was fine!

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:11.020
She could have gone.

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:12.570
- Is that your opinion, Doctor?

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:15.407
Wait, that's right!

00:04:15.407 --> 00:04:17.670
You're not a doctor.

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:21.520
- Tension between doctors
and administrators

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:23.100
definitely exists.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:25.660
I wouldn't say it's this bad.

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:27.710
Generally, though, everyone has

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:29.400
the patient's best interest at heart.

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:31.410
Not everyone is so money
hungry as they're making

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:32.780
this gentleman out to be.

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:35.450
They definitely think about
profits more than they should,

00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:37.330
but I don't think they would ever

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:38.890
put in jeopardy a child's life.

00:04:38.890 --> 00:04:40.070
Especially a baby's life.

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:42.610
The difficult part about
being a hospital administrator

00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:47.300
is that you are constantly
forced to make a decision.

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:50.320
Let's say keeping the baby
will cost you so much money,

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:53.410
these type of losses will
become larger and larger,

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:55.200
and then the hospital will
be forced to close down,

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:57.350
you're hurting thousands of people.

00:04:57.350 --> 00:04:58.250
If not, millions of people.

00:04:58.250 --> 00:04:59.083
I don't know how many people

00:04:59.083 --> 00:05:00.080
have come to this hospital, but.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:01.410
It's a tough decision.

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:03.560
It's not an easy position to be in.

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:05.290
So, I don't envy that job.

00:05:05.290 --> 00:05:07.560
But, like I always say, be a human first.

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:09.450
Hospital administrator or
doctor or whoever you are.

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:10.360
Human first.

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:11.891
- [T.C.] Welcome to our break room!

00:05:11.891 --> 00:05:14.000
(uptempo Country music)

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:15.206
- [Paul] Look at this place!

00:05:15.206 --> 00:05:17.003
It's insane!

00:05:17.003 --> 00:05:18.900
- (laughs) This is awesome!

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:20.600
- I want a break room like that!

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:21.433
What?!

00:05:21.433 --> 00:05:23.800
In one hospital, we had a ping pong table

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:25.990
and nobody wanted to leave that room.

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:28.740
They have a full functioning
bar and a basketball hoop?!

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:29.754
That's amazing!

00:05:29.754 --> 00:05:31.090
- Guys, we got a GSW!

00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:31.970
Drew, you're up!

00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:33.430
- Gunshot wound.

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:34.870
- BP and blood rate dropping.

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:35.810
- [Jordan] All right,
what's happening in here?

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:36.650
- Simple GSW.

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:37.880
I got the bullet, but he's still bleeding.

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:38.940
You check for fragments?

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:39.970
- I got the whole bullet.

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:40.860
- Bone fragments.

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:43.150
- When you have a gunshot
wound, taking out the bullet

00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:46.620
is not the most important
aspect of treating someone.

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:49.610
Because just taking out
the bullet doesn't stop

00:05:49.610 --> 00:05:51.510
the problem that's going
on with the patient

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:52.690
and that's bleeding.

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:54.140
So, you need to control the bleeding

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:55.440
and in order to control the bleeding,

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:56.960
you need to find the
source of the bleeding.

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:00.080
So, pulling out the bullet is one step.

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:01.610
But, there's a lot of people

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:04.069
that still have bullets even in them.

00:06:04.069 --> 00:06:07.100
Because we stop the bleeding
and there is no medical need

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:08.050
to take out the bullet.

00:06:08.050 --> 00:06:10.110
- If we keep her, what do
I tell the next 50 people

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:12.221
who come in here that we
cannot afford to treat?

00:06:12.221 --> 00:06:13.250
- I'll treat them.

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:14.083
- God, T.C.!

00:06:14.083 --> 00:06:15.050
Did you not learn anything

00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:16.381
from being kicked out of the army?

00:06:16.381 --> 00:06:17.214
(groans)

00:06:17.214 --> 00:06:20.550
You are an amazing doctor,
but for some reason,

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:22.400
they thought that they
were better off without you

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:23.750
during a war!

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:25.013
What does that tell you?

00:06:25.013 --> 00:06:27.280
- They care more about
politics than lives.

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:31.680
- The problem with being too altruistic,

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:34.250
and there is such thing
as being too altruistic,

00:06:34.250 --> 00:06:37.230
is you become an idealist
and at times as a doctor,

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:38.860
you can become an idealist.

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:40.720
You have to be rational,
you have to be practical,

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:42.090
you have to be quick thinking.

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:45.500
If you constantly put
your hospital in the red

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:48.220
because you either over-order a test

00:06:49.518 --> 00:06:51.570
or you perform procedures

00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:54.380
that won't be covered by insurances,

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:56.700
you could bankrupt the hospital system

00:06:56.700 --> 00:06:58.230
and force the hospital system to close

00:06:58.230 --> 00:07:00.262
and then hurt so many more people.

00:07:00.262 --> 00:07:01.979
(female patient winces)

00:07:01.979 --> 00:07:02.812
- Sorry.

00:07:02.812 --> 00:07:05.090
- Has it always been this
size or has it gotten bigger?

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:05.990
- [Female Patient] Uh, it's gotten bigger

00:07:05.990 --> 00:07:07.375
in the last few years.

00:07:07.375 --> 00:07:08.990
- [Topher] Whoa!

00:07:08.990 --> 00:07:10.100
- Look, I'm sorry to bother you with this.

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:11.795
It's just been hurting so much lately.

00:07:11.795 --> 00:07:13.973
It's been hard to work.

00:07:13.973 --> 00:07:15.473
- What have other doctors say?

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:19.510
- That I don't have insurance.

00:07:19.510 --> 00:07:22.920
- I work in Atlantic Health
System Overlook Medical Center

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:25.410
where we have a fantastic
charity care system.

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:28.840
That if someone makes a
small amount of money,

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:31.760
is uninsured, they can
almost be seen for free.

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:33.400
I mean, some of the charges,

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:35.410
like, to see a doctor, is like, $10 or $5.

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:36.243
They're very low.

00:07:36.243 --> 00:07:38.910
If you're faced with this
dilemma, you don't have insurance,

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:40.110
you think you should get checked out

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:42.553
or you wanna get check
out, it doesn't mean

00:07:42.553 --> 00:07:44.040
because you don't insurance,

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:45.660
nobody's gonna take care of you.

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:49.690
Figure out a way to get
seen, talk to social workers,

00:07:49.690 --> 00:07:51.860
talk to government employees.

00:07:51.860 --> 00:07:54.210
There's usually a program that we can tap

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:55.470
to get you the help that you need.

00:07:55.470 --> 00:07:58.360
- T.C. drives me nuts, but
there's no shock there.

00:07:58.360 --> 00:07:59.270
- Oh.

00:07:59.270 --> 00:08:00.571
You two don't get along?

00:08:00.571 --> 00:08:02.570
- (sighs) It's complicated.

00:08:02.570 --> 00:08:03.969
- Sounds like an interesting story.

00:08:03.969 --> 00:08:04.802
(laughs)

00:08:04.802 --> 00:08:05.635
- Oh, it's a long one.

00:08:06.645 --> 00:08:09.420
♪ Romance drama incoming ♪

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:10.780
- Well, if the patient can't pay,

00:08:10.780 --> 00:08:12.290
then they get the minimum treatment.

00:08:12.290 --> 00:08:14.030
They just cost us too much money.

00:08:14.030 --> 00:08:15.440
- Well, if all you care about is money,

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:16.700
I can save you a ton.

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:17.940
Just shut down the hospital.

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:19.580
Then, you won't have to treat anybody.

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:21.170
- Well, if we keep hemorrhaging losses,

00:08:21.170 --> 00:08:22.530
they will shut this place down.

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:23.363
- Oh, come on.

00:08:23.363 --> 00:08:25.150
- I don't usually agree
with administrators,

00:08:25.150 --> 00:08:26.100
but I agree here.

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:29.670
In Afghanistan or in a war
zone, you have no choice!

00:08:29.670 --> 00:08:32.440
If you don't act, this person dies.

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:35.070
So, even if you're not giving
them the best quality care,

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:37.020
you're doing the best that
you can in that moment

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:38.150
to save their life.

00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:41.080
But, in a time where you pull
up on the side of an accident

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:45.000
non-sterile, the best thing
you can do for the patient

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:46.920
is get them to a hospital
as soon as possible.

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:49.213
Not to perform surgery in the woods.

00:08:52.970 --> 00:08:55.469
- T.C., you're not as tough
as people say you are.

00:08:55.469 --> 00:08:58.010
(loud punch)

00:08:58.010 --> 00:09:00.920
- That's a fireable
offense in any hospital.

00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:02.530
So, I'm curious what's gonna happen next.

00:09:02.530 --> 00:09:03.363
'Cause he went.

00:09:03.363 --> 00:09:04.196
(loud smack)

00:09:04.196 --> 00:09:05.029
Bam!

00:09:05.029 --> 00:09:06.118
- Okay, we're gonna take care of you.

00:09:06.118 --> 00:09:08.010
Someone's gonna get you out of here.

00:09:08.010 --> 00:09:10.223
Pull me up!
- How bad?

00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:13.890
- His skull is separated
from the spinal column.

00:09:13.890 --> 00:09:15.580
I can see 'em moving
separately with each breath.

00:09:15.580 --> 00:09:16.840
- [Topher] Oh my god.

00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:18.060
How is he still alive?

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:18.960
- By some miracle.

00:09:19.910 --> 00:09:21.360
- Drama really does live here.

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:24.840
Um, saying that his skull is
separated from his spinal cord

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:27.540
and that he sees it
moving with each breath,

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:28.890
I've never heard this!

00:09:28.890 --> 00:09:30.450
It sounds dramatic.

00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:31.950
It is dramatic.

00:09:31.950 --> 00:09:32.783
Could it happen?

00:09:32.783 --> 00:09:33.616
Yes.

00:09:33.616 --> 00:09:34.780
The fact that he diagnosed it so quickly

00:09:34.780 --> 00:09:36.853
in this type of situation is impressive.

00:09:37.750 --> 00:09:39.640
- We gotta get out of here
in the next 10 minutes.

00:09:39.640 --> 00:09:41.350
A storm is movin' in!

00:09:41.350 --> 00:09:43.063
- Okay, let's do this.

00:09:44.230 --> 00:09:45.930
- There wasn't enough drama.

00:09:45.930 --> 00:09:47.316
They add a storm coming.

00:09:47.316 --> 00:09:48.430
- When we cut this thing open.

00:09:48.430 --> 00:09:49.777
- I gotta keep still.

00:09:49.777 --> 00:09:52.183
- And maybe the car will light on fire.

00:09:54.603 --> 00:09:56.590
(laughs) And maybe an
active shooter situation

00:09:56.590 --> 00:09:58.270
will start taking place.

00:09:58.270 --> 00:09:59.910
I feel like whatever can go wrong

00:09:59.910 --> 00:10:01.570
is gonna go wrong in this situation.

00:10:01.570 --> 00:10:03.420
- You can't fire him
because medically speaking,

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:04.720
he is irreplaceable.

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:06.200
- Everybody can be replaced!

00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:07.860
- If you hit anybody,

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:10.470
let alone a hospital
administrator, you're fired.

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:11.303
You should be fired.

00:10:11.303 --> 00:10:12.600
That means you can't control yourself.

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:14.673
And no self-control makes a bad doctor.

00:10:14.673 --> 00:10:16.130
I don't care how talented you are.

00:10:16.130 --> 00:10:16.963
- Come on.

00:10:16.963 --> 00:10:18.410
Let's do this before someone
walks in on us, please.

00:10:18.410 --> 00:10:19.351
- What are they doing?

00:10:19.351 --> 00:10:20.302
(high pitched ringing)

00:10:20.302 --> 00:10:21.135
- Clear.

00:10:21.135 --> 00:10:21.968
- Clear.
- Clear.

00:10:21.968 --> 00:10:22.801
(loud bang)

00:10:22.801 --> 00:10:23.634
(loud gasp)

00:10:23.634 --> 00:10:24.467
- All right.

00:10:24.467 --> 00:10:25.300
There we go.

00:10:25.300 --> 00:10:26.133
Trust me, you don't want your first time

00:10:26.133 --> 00:10:27.200
beating on someone who really needs it.

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:28.267
Paul, you're up.

00:10:28.267 --> 00:10:30.865
- (laughs) We don't shock
dead patients for practice.

00:10:30.865 --> 00:10:31.810
(sighs)

00:10:31.810 --> 00:10:34.333
- You did a spinal
decapitation surgery in a tent.

00:10:35.248 --> 00:10:36.081
(laughs)

00:10:36.081 --> 00:10:36.914
I'm impressed.

00:10:36.914 --> 00:10:38.150
- Yeah?
- Yes, I am.

00:10:38.150 --> 00:10:39.034
Very.

00:10:39.034 --> 00:10:42.470
(sighs and laughs)

00:10:42.470 --> 00:10:44.323
- I really thought he was gonna make it.

00:10:45.550 --> 00:10:46.850
- Oh my gosh.

00:10:46.850 --> 00:10:48.780
I don't know why I'm so
frustrated with this show.

00:10:48.780 --> 00:10:50.750
He was a doctor in Afghanistan,

00:10:50.750 --> 00:10:54.860
had performed neurosurgical
procedures/surgeries,

00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:57.250
and was bad at them because
he wasn't trained to do them?

00:10:57.250 --> 00:10:58.850
It doesn't sound surprising to me.

00:10:58.850 --> 00:11:00.040
I don't follow the logic.

00:11:00.040 --> 00:11:01.790
This show is not for medical folks.

00:11:01.790 --> 00:11:03.590
- I know we're not together anymore,

00:11:05.777 --> 00:11:09.040
but it doesn't mean that I've
stopped caring about you.

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:10.010
- She woves him.

00:11:10.010 --> 00:11:11.350
- Unbelievable!

00:11:11.350 --> 00:11:14.700
This guy's got a .3 alcohol
level, creams another car,

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:16.760
and he only ends up with a
black eye and a boo-boo neck.

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:17.837
Get him in recovery.

00:11:17.837 --> 00:11:21.590
- .3 alcohol level is
very, very, very high.

00:11:21.590 --> 00:11:22.423
Just so you're aware.

00:11:22.423 --> 00:11:23.350
Very, very high.

00:11:23.350 --> 00:11:26.200
I'm talking about, like, a
bottle and a half of vodka high.

00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:27.190
- This button.

00:11:27.190 --> 00:11:30.050
And your hand controls the
morphine from this machine

00:11:30.050 --> 00:11:31.087
that's next to your bed.

00:11:31.087 --> 00:11:33.746
It's to help ease your pain.

00:11:33.746 --> 00:11:34.579
- Oh.

00:11:35.830 --> 00:11:36.663
- Button.

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:39.253
Morphine.

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:41.073
Pain.

00:11:44.637 --> 00:11:45.670
- That's not right.

00:11:45.670 --> 00:11:47.600
People make mistakes.

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:49.650
You have to treat everyone whether or not

00:11:49.650 --> 00:11:51.340
they're a criminal, not a criminal.

00:11:51.340 --> 00:11:53.640
It's not your job to figure
out who's right and who's wrong

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:54.700
in any situation.

00:11:54.700 --> 00:11:56.600
As a doctor, you take the Hippocratic Oath

00:11:56.600 --> 00:11:58.290
for that exact reason.

00:11:58.290 --> 00:12:01.800
You will treat anybody regardless
of their race, religion,

00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:03.650
criminal status, not criminal status,

00:12:03.650 --> 00:12:04.850
war criminal, not war criminal.

00:12:04.850 --> 00:12:05.830
Everybody.

00:12:05.830 --> 00:12:08.390
You can't be deciding and playing God

00:12:08.390 --> 00:12:10.370
who you are and you aren't saving.

00:12:10.370 --> 00:12:11.550
- Just take a nap.

00:12:11.550 --> 00:12:13.420
Okay, you need to be very still.

00:12:13.420 --> 00:12:14.670
We only have one shot at this.

00:12:14.670 --> 00:12:15.503
- When you take a nap,

00:12:15.503 --> 00:12:16.410
I don't know if it's ever happened to you.

00:12:16.410 --> 00:12:17.660
It happens to me all the time.

00:12:17.660 --> 00:12:18.830
You get, like, a little chivalrous

00:12:18.830 --> 00:12:19.800
when you're falling asleep.

00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:20.960
And that's really bad for an MRI

00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:22.540
'cause you have to stay very still.

00:12:22.540 --> 00:12:25.010
So, taking a nap is
not always good advice.

00:12:25.010 --> 00:12:26.830
- I loved the army team

00:12:26.830 --> 00:12:29.443
and I will not risk
losing that for anything.

00:12:31.580 --> 00:12:32.980
Coming out is not an option.

00:12:35.268 --> 00:12:37.860
(heavy sigh)

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:38.827
- That is so gay.

00:12:39.777 --> 00:12:40.610
(chuckles)

00:12:40.610 --> 00:12:42.460
- Your sexual preference
doesn't define you.

00:12:42.460 --> 00:12:44.450
It doesn't define the
type of person you are.

00:12:44.450 --> 00:12:45.283
You're a doctor.

00:12:45.283 --> 00:12:46.540
Your job is to be a doctor.

00:12:46.540 --> 00:12:47.880
All of those things don't matter.

00:12:47.880 --> 00:12:49.520
If you like women, if you like men,

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:52.180
if you like heavier people,
if you like skinnier people.

00:12:52.180 --> 00:12:54.440
It doesn't matter if
you're good at your job

00:12:54.440 --> 00:12:56.780
and you follow rules and
you don't slap people!

00:12:56.780 --> 00:12:59.420
- I loved hearing from my new
boss that I'm banging her ex.

00:12:59.420 --> 00:13:00.253
- Mm.

00:13:00.253 --> 00:13:01.160
- Thanks for the heads up on that one.

00:13:01.160 --> 00:13:01.993
- Yeah.

00:13:02.901 --> 00:13:06.720
- And here, I thought you were a virgin.

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:08.540
(laughing)

00:13:08.540 --> 00:13:10.280
- And the sex returns!

00:13:10.280 --> 00:13:11.503
- Fetus in fetu.

00:13:12.510 --> 00:13:16.273
It's gonna sound freaky,
but you had a twin.

00:13:17.140 --> 00:13:19.330
- A twin?
- Yes.

00:13:19.330 --> 00:13:22.190
At your conception or early on,

00:13:22.190 --> 00:13:24.560
your twin didn't develop,
but it stayed alive

00:13:24.560 --> 00:13:28.160
in a very small tumor-sized
form inside your body.

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:31.370
- I can just imagine the pitch
meeting for this TV show.

00:13:31.370 --> 00:13:34.210
It's like, we need a very rare
condition for this young girl

00:13:34.210 --> 00:13:38.130
to have where she has some
sort of other human being

00:13:38.130 --> 00:13:39.250
living inside of her.

00:13:39.250 --> 00:13:41.050
I've heard of stories like this before,

00:13:41.050 --> 00:13:44.110
but they were just a case study
which just means one person

00:13:44.110 --> 00:13:46.150
out of millions that have this condition.

00:13:46.150 --> 00:13:49.060
It's a pretty rare condition
where basically the tumor is.

00:13:49.060 --> 00:13:52.922
Your twin that died but didn't
survive still lives inside

00:13:52.922 --> 00:13:54.310
in this little tumor.

00:13:54.310 --> 00:13:55.300
Well, lives is a bad term.

00:13:55.300 --> 00:13:58.870
There's also tumors that
exist known as teratomas

00:13:58.870 --> 00:14:03.410
which basically arise from
the cells in your body

00:14:03.410 --> 00:14:05.620
which are responsible for growing

00:14:05.620 --> 00:14:07.860
specific other types of cells.

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:10.910
So, within a teratoma, it's not
unusual to find fingernails,

00:14:10.910 --> 00:14:12.870
hair, really abnormal stuff

00:14:12.870 --> 00:14:14.550
and these teratomas can form anywhere.

00:14:14.550 --> 00:14:17.547
Imagine finding teeth in your arm.

00:14:17.547 --> 00:14:19.260
That's what a teratoma is like.

00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:20.093
It's pretty crazy.

00:14:20.093 --> 00:14:24.250
- We should have a conversation
about what happened earlier.

00:14:24.250 --> 00:14:25.266
- Yeah.

00:14:25.266 --> 00:14:26.099
(loud punch)

00:14:26.099 --> 00:14:26.932
(grunts)

00:14:26.932 --> 00:14:27.765
(loud inhale)

00:14:27.765 --> 00:14:28.920
(chuckles)

00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:30.080
- Now, we're even.

00:14:30.080 --> 00:14:31.980
- This is a cool hospital where people

00:14:31.980 --> 00:14:34.163
just slap and punch
each other in the face.

00:14:35.889 --> 00:14:36.722
(chuckles)

00:14:36.722 --> 00:14:38.420
You're not really even
'cause you got knocked out

00:14:38.420 --> 00:14:41.180
from a slap and you
punched him and he laughed.

00:14:41.180 --> 00:14:43.570
- I had a meeting with Ragosa.

00:14:43.570 --> 00:14:44.420
We worked it out.

00:14:46.420 --> 00:14:47.288
I ain't goin' anywhere.

00:14:47.288 --> 00:14:49.805
(everyone claps and cheers)

00:14:49.805 --> 00:14:52.710
- Well, that was not a
medically accurate show,

00:14:52.710 --> 00:14:55.560
but it was quite entertaining,
lighthearted fun.

00:14:55.560 --> 00:14:57.460
And I could see why a lot
of people would enjoy it.

00:14:57.460 --> 00:15:00.380
So, I appreciate you guys
sending me the recommendation.

00:15:00.380 --> 00:15:02.550
I will have to say that there's
not a lot for me to comment

00:15:02.550 --> 00:15:05.730
medically on this episode
because the conditions

00:15:05.730 --> 00:15:09.800
that occurred here were
very dramatic and rare.

00:15:09.800 --> 00:15:11.580
The way that they perform
some of these things

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:15.990
is just not realistic and
not how most hospitals

00:15:15.990 --> 00:15:18.880
would handle things and
administrators would handle things.

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:20.890
But, you know, it's a fun upbeat show

00:15:20.890 --> 00:15:23.830
and it's like a hoorah
army show and I dig that.

00:15:23.830 --> 00:15:25.660
I'm all about supporting the troops.

00:15:25.660 --> 00:15:28.380
If there's an episode that
